## Frequently Asked Questions

### What color grade should I get for asscher cut diamond stud earrings?

For lab asscher studs, the catalog ships F-G color — appropriate and ideal; F-G in white or yellow gold faces up clean and white at ear distance. For natural asscher studs, the catalog ships H-I color. H-I in white gold shows visible warmth in the step-cut facets at conversational distance due to the Forward-Face Effect; yellow gold partially absorbs the warmth and is the better metal choice for H-I natural pairs.

### What clarity grade is acceptable for asscher cut stud earrings?

VS2 is the minimum clarity for asscher studs — this is what lab pairs ship at, and it is eye-clean at conversational distance for most buyers. SI1 inclusions are often visible to the naked eye in the asscher's large parallel facets at 6-12 inches of direct forward viewing. Natural asscher studs ship at SI1-SI2; the inclusions carry real visibility risk in the earring format given the Forward-Face Effect.

### What does "total carat weight" (tw) mean for diamond stud earrings?

Total carat weight is the combined weight of both stones in the pair — a 1ct tw pair has two diamonds, each approximately 0.50ct. A 1/2ct tw pair has two diamonds, each approximately 0.25ct. When comparing earring pairs, convert to per-ear carat weight to understand the individual stone size; the tables in this guide show the per-ear estimate for every option.

### Are lab-grown asscher cut studs the same quality as natural?

Lab asscher studs in this catalog grade F-G color and VS2-SI1 clarity — a higher standard than the natural H-I/SI1-SI2 pairs in the same catalog. Lab-grown diamonds are chemically and optically identical to natural diamonds; the difference is origin, not appearance. GIA and IGI grading applies equally to lab and natural stones — F-G lab and F-G natural are the same color grade.

### What is the Mirror Pair Problem for asscher stud earrings?

The Mirror Pair Problem describes the visual risk of wearing two step-cut windows simultaneously — both asscher stud Hall of Mirrors windows face the viewer at face level at the same time. Color or clarity variation between the two stones shows in the side-by-side step-cut comparison at conversational distance; this problem does not exist in round brilliant pairs. Lab pairs mitigate this by shipping from matched F-G/VS2-SI1 lots; natural H-I pairs may vary within the H-I range.

### What is the Forward-Face Effect for asscher studs?

The Forward-Face Effect describes how asscher studs sit against the earlobe with the step-cut table facing directly outward toward the viewer — at face level, 6-12 inches away in normal conversation. Unlike a pendant at 18" or a ring at arm distance, the asscher stud's Hall of Mirrors faces the viewer head-on. Color warmth and inclusions that might be partly absorbed in other formats are clearly visible in the asscher stud's forward-facing step-cut geometry.

### Should I get a screw-back or push-back backing for asscher studs?

Standard asscher studs ship with push-back posts, which are appropriate for most buyers and most activities. For pairs at 1ct tw ($828 lab or $5,750 natural) and above, a screw-back backing adds meaningful security — it requires twisting to release and makes accidental loss significantly harder. A local jeweler can upgrade the backing after purchase if you want added security.

### What metal should I choose for asscher cut stud earrings?

For lab F-G/VS2-SI1 pairs, 14K white gold is the strongest choice — the white metal shows the clean color most clearly at face level. For natural H-I/SI1-SI2 pairs, 14K yellow gold is the better choice; the warm metal partially absorbs the H-I stone's color warmth. Platinum (natural pairs only) adds $100-350 over white gold per size and eliminates rhodium replating — a real advantage for daily wear.

### How do asscher cut studs compare to round brilliant studs?

Round brilliant studs scatter light in all directions — color warmth and inclusions blend into sparkle at ear distance; asscher studs have step-cut facets that face the viewer directly and show geometric depth. The asscher stud shows quality attributes more directly; the round brilliant is more forgiving of lower grades. Asscher studs are considerably rarer and more architecturally distinctive.

### Are asscher cut studs appropriate for everyday wear?

Yes — asscher studs are practical for everyday wear at all sizes; the stud format has no prong-catching issues common to ring settings and no length complications from pendant format. Earrings have lower mechanical stress than rings, making daily wear appropriate from 1/4ct tw through 3ct tw. For valuable pairs at 1ct tw and above, consider a screw-back backing upgrade for added security.

### What is the best total carat weight for asscher stud earrings?

For subtle everyday wear where the shape is the interest: 1/4ct to 1/2ct tw. For clear visibility at conversational distance where the step-cut geometry fully reads: 1/2ct to 1ct tw. For statement wear where the earrings are the focal point: 1ct tw and above — at 2ct tw (1ct per ear), the asscher stud is a bold architectural choice that is immediately visible and extremely rare in most social contexts.

### How much do asscher cut diamond stud earrings cost?

Lab asscher studs range from $438 (1/4ct tw) to $1,746 (3ct tw) from live certified inventory. Natural asscher studs range from $900 (1/4ct tw) to $13,540 (2ct tw platinum) — at every size, lab delivers better color and clarity at a lower price. The starkest comparison is at 1ct tw: lab $828 vs natural $5,750, a $4,922 difference for a pair that grades better on both color and clarity.

### Can I buy a single asscher stud to replace a lost earring?

The catalog sells matched pairs only. To replace a single lost earring, you would need to source a single stone and setting separately, then have both matched by a jeweler for color and size consistency — the Mirror Pair Problem makes this matching critical for step-cut earrings. Working with a jeweler who specializes in stone matching is important for any step-cut stud replacement.

### Do asscher cut studs work for a curated ear look?

Yes — asscher studs are flat against the earlobe, which makes them a clean base for a curated ear. They layer well with ear cuffs, huggie hoops, and smaller studs in higher ear positions; the flat geometric profile does not project forward like a drop earring. The asscher's octagonal shape pairs particularly well with simple geometric ear cuffs — the angular shapes reinforce each other.

### What is the best asscher stud size to match an asscher engagement ring?

For matching asscher studs to an asscher ring, the 1/2ct to 1ct tw lab pair in white gold is the standard recommendation. The stud size does not need to match the ring stone size — the earrings are seen at face level while the ring is seen at hand level, so a smaller stud reads as proportionally appropriate. Color matching matters more than size matching: choose F-G lab studs to align with a D-F or G-H ring stone rather than choosing H-I natural studs that may read as visibly different in quality.

### Is there a quality difference between 14K and 18K gold for earring posts?

The catalog offers 14K gold — not 18K. 14K gold (58.3% pure) is more durable for earring posts than 18K (75% pure), which is softer and more prone to bending at the post with daily wearing and removal. For studs used every day, 14K is the practical durability choice. Platinum posts are the most durable option in the catalog and require no rhodium replating.
